## Proof

Let `Λ` be a lattice of rank `d` with volume `Vol(Λ)`, and let BKZ with block size `k` (equipped with an exact SVP oracle on each `k`-dimensional projected block) be run to completion. We derive the expected norm of the first output vector `b₁`.

### 1. The block-level guarantee

Inside any `k`-dimensional projected sublattice `Λ_i` with volume `Vol(Λ_i)`, the SVP oracle returns a shortest vector. By definition of the Hermite constant `γ_k`, the squared length of this shortest vector satisfies

```
||v||² = γ_k · Vol(Λ_i)^(2/k).
```

Equivalently, the *root Hermite factor* associated with block size `k` is

```
δ_k := γ_k^(1/(2(k−1))).
```

This `δ_k` depends only on `k` (asymptotically `δ_k ≈ (k/(2πe))^(1/(2(k−1)))` for large `k`), and it is the per-block reduction quality that BKZ propagates through the whole basis.

### 2. Geometric Series Assumption (GSA)

After BKZ-`k` terminates, the Gram–Schmidt lengths `||b_i*||` of the reduced basis are modeled (and empirically observed) to form a geometric progression. Writing the common ratio in terms of `δ_k`, the GSA states

```
||b_i*|| = δ_k^(d − 2i + 1) · Vol(Λ)^(1/d),     i = 1, …, d.
```

This is consistent with the block guarantee: in the trailing block of size `k` one has `||b_{d−k+1}*|| ≈ δ_k^(2k−1)·Vol(Λ)^(1/d)`, and the per-block SVP condition `||b_{d−k+1}*||^k ≈ γ_k · Vol(block)` is exactly the relation `δ_k^(2(k−1)) = γ_k` rewritten through the geometric chain.

### 3. Norm of the output vector

Since `b₁` is already orthogonal to all later vectors, `||b₁|| = ||b₁*||`. Setting `i = 1` in the GSA formula,

```
||b₁|| = δ_k^(d − 1) · Vol(Λ)^(1/d).
```

Taking the expectation over the randomness of the SVP oracle (and of the lattice, when viewed as random), the expected norm of the output vector is therefore

```
E[||b₁||] ≈ δ_k^(d − 1) · Vol(Λ)^(1/d).
```

### 4. Conclusion

The expected norm of the output vector produced by BKZ with block size `k` and an SVP oracle, on a rank-`d` lattice of volume `Vol(Λ)`, is

$$
\boxed{\;\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|b_1\|\bigr] \;\approx\; \delta_k^{\,d-1}\,\operatorname{Vol}(\Lambda)^{1/d}\;}
$$

where `δ_k = γ_k^{1/(2(k−1))}` is the root Hermite factor determined by the Hermite constant `γ_k` of dimension `k`. The exponent `d−1` (rather than `d`) reflects that the geometric progression of Gram–Schmidt lengths spans `d−1` ratios between the `d` basis vectors.

